analysisWe need to produce an assert statement for the comment:

```java
// @@@ The length of the returned array is equal to the size of the vector.
```

Method toArray returns Object[].

We need to assert that the length of the returned array equals the size of the vector after operation, i.e., after toArray call. Use \result for return value. Use this.size() (observer method) for size after execution. Since size is a public method (synchronized) returning int.

Thus assert: assert \result.length == this.size();

Java syntax for array length: .length.

So final answer: <code>assert \result.length == this.size();</code>

Check if need to refer to old size? The comment says "length of the returned array is equal to the size of the vector." That's after the call, size hasn't changed (toArray doesn't modify). So using this.size() after call is fine. Could also use \old(this.size()) but not necessary. Usually they use after. To be safe, use \old(this.size())? The vector size unchanged, but spec expects equality with size after call. Many examples used this.getCount() after add. So use this.size().

